      "text": "To find the distance between the foci of the given ellipse, we need to determine the parameters of the ellipse. The equation of the ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal major axis is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} = 1\n\\]\n\nFrom the given graph, we can identify the lengths of the semi-major axis \\(a\\) and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\):\n\n- The length of the semi-major axis \\(a\\) is 6.\n- The length of the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is 2.\n\nThe distance between the foci of an ellipse is given by \\(2c\\), where \\(c\\) is the focal distance computed using the relationship:\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\n\\[\nc = \\sqrt{6^2 - 2^2} = \\sqrt{36 - 4} = \\sqrt{32} = 4\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the foci is:\n\n\\[\n2c = 2 \\times 4\\sqrt{2} = 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the distance between the foci is \\(\\boxed{8\\sqrt{2}}\\).",
