      "text": "1. **Find the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes:**\n\n   The equation of the ellipse given is \\((x',y')\\) scaled version.\n   The equation for the ellipse is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(x(x')^2 + y(y')^2)}{(x')^2 + (y')^2} = 1\n   \\]\n\n   We know the center, which is the origin \\((0,0)\\).\n\n2. **Identify the lengths of the semi-major axis \\(a\\) and semi-minor axis \\(b\\):**\n\n   The semi-major axis \\(a\\) is the longest semi-axis, and the semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is the shortest one.\n   The semi-major axis is half the length of the line segment from \\((1,1)\\) to the origin.\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{\\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2}}{\\sqrt{1}} = \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The semi-minor axis \\(b\\) is the length of the line segment from the origin to the farthest point from the focus in the \\(y\\)-direction.\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{\\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2}}{\\sqrt{1}} = \\sqrt{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the coordinates of the foci:**\n\n   For an ellipse, the distance from the center to each focus is given by:\n   \\[\n   c = \\sqrt{a^2 - b^2}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Substitute the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):**\n   \\[\n   c = \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^2 - (\\sqrt{2})^2} = \\sqrt{2 - 2} = \\sqrt{0} = 0\n   \\]\n\n   The distance between the foci is \\(2c\\).\n\n5. **Calculate the distance between the foci:**\n\n   Since \\(c = 0\\), the distance between the foci is:\n   \\[\n   2c = 2 \\times 0 = 0\n   \\]\n\nConclusion:\nThe distance between the foci is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).",
